ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan
==================================================
Manhattan [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-9]  is the most densely populated and geographically smallest of the  [LINK: (five boroughs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boroughs_of_New_York_City]  of  [LINK: (New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City] . Coextensive with New York County, Manhattan is the  [LINK: (smallest county by area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_statistics_of_the_United_States#Smallest,_largest,_and_average_area_per_state_and_territory]  in the  [LINK: (U.S. state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._state]  of  [LINK: (New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_(state)] , and one of the  [LINK: (smallest in the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_United_States_counties_and_county_equivalents] . Located almost entirely on Manhattan Island near the southern tip of the state, Manhattan is centrally located in the  [LINK: (Northeast megalopolis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northeast_megalopolis]  and represents the urban core of the  [LINK: (New York metropolitan area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_metropolitan_area] . [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-10]  Manhattan serves as New York City's  [LINK: (economic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economy_of_New_York_City]  and  [LINK: (administrative) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_New_York_City]  center and has been described as the cultural, financial,  [LINK: (media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_in_New_York_City] , and  [LINK: (entertainment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Show_business]  capital of the world. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-11]  [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-12]  [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-13]  [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ManhattanMediaEntertainmentCapital-14]

Before European colonization, present-day Manhattan was part of  [LINK: (Lenape) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenape]  territory. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-:1-15]  European settlement began with the establishment of a  [LINK: (trading post) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trading_post]  by  [LINK: (Dutch colonists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_colonization_of_the_Americas]  in 1624 on Manhattan Island; the post was named  [LINK: (New Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Amsterdam]  in 1626. The territory came under English control in 1664 and was renamed New York after King  [LINK: (Charles II of England) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_II_of_England]  granted the lands to his brother, the  [LINK: (Duke of York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_II_of_England] . [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-16]  New York, based in present-day  [LINK: (Lower Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Manhattan] , served as the  [LINK: (capital of the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_capitals_in_the_United_States#Capitals_of_the_US]  from 1785 until 1790. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-senate-17]  The  [LINK: (Statue of Liberty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statue_of_Liberty]  in  [LINK: (New York Harbor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Harbor]  greeted millions of arriving immigrants  [LINK: (in the late 19th century) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_immigration_to_the_United_States#1850_to_1930]  and is a world symbol of the United States and its ideals. [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-18]  Manhattan became a borough during the  [LINK: (consolidation of New York City in 1898) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consolidation_of_New_York_City] , and houses  [LINK: (New York City Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Hall] , the seat of the  [LINK: (city's government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_New_York_City] . [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-19]   [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem]  in  [LINK: (Upper Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_Manhattan]  became the center of what is now known as the cultural  [LINK: (Harlem Renaissance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_Renaissance]  in the 1920s. The  [LINK: (Stonewall Inn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_Inn]  in  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village] , part of the  [LINK: (Stonewall National Monument) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_National_Monument] , is considered the  [LINK: (birthplace in 1969) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_riots]  of the modern  [LINK: (gay-rights movement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBTQ_movements] , cementing Manhattan's central role in  [LINK: (LGBTQ culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBTQ_culture_in_New_York_City#Manhattan] . [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NPS99000562-20]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ObamaStonewall-21]  Manhattan was the site of the original  [LINK: (World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(1973%E2%80%932001)] , which was  [LINK: (destroyed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collapse_of_the_World_Trade_Center]  during the  [LINK: (September 11 terrorist attacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_attacks]  in 2001.

Situated on  [LINK: (one of the world's largest natural harbors) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Harbor] , the borough is bounded by the  [LINK: (Hudson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River] ,  [LINK: (East) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_River] , and  [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_River]  rivers and includes  [LINK: (several small adjacent islands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_smaller_islands_in_New_York_City] , including  [LINK: (Roosevelt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roosevelt_Island] ,  [LINK: (U Thant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U_Thant_Island] , and  [LINK: (Randalls and Wards Islands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands] . It also includes the small neighborhood of  [LINK: (Marble Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Hill,_Manhattan] , now on the  [LINK: (U.S. mainland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._mainland] . Manhattan Island is divided into three informally bounded components, each cutting across the borough's long axis: Lower Manhattan,  [LINK: (Midtown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midtown_Manhattan] , and Upper Manhattan. Manhattan is one of the most densely populated locations in the world, with a  [LINK: (2020 census) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_census]  population of 1,694,250 living in a land area of 22.66 square miles (58.69 km2), [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-QuickFacts-5]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-22]  or 72,918 residents per square mile (28,154 residents/km2), and its residential property has the highest sale price per square foot in the United States. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ManhattanPricePerSquareFoot-23]

Manhattan is home to  [LINK: (Wall Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall_Street]  as well as the world's two  [LINK: (largest stock exchanges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_stock_exchanges]  by total  [LINK: (market capitalization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_capitalization] , the  [LINK: (New York Stock Exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Stock_Exchange]  and  [LINK: (Nasdaq) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq] . [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-LargestExchanges-24]  Many multinational  [LINK: (media conglomerates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_conglomerate]  are based in Manhattan, as are numerous colleges and universities, such as  [LINK: (Columbia University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University] ,  [LINK: (New York University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_University] ,  [LINK: (Rockefeller University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockefeller_University] , and the  [LINK: (City University of New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_University_of_New_York] . The  [LINK: (headquarters of the United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headquarters_of_the_United_Nations]  is located in the  [LINK: (Turtle Bay) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle_Bay,_Manhattan]  neighborhood of Midtown Manhattan. Manhattan hosts three of the world's top 10 most-visited tourist attractions:  [LINK: (Times Square) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Times_Square] ,  [LINK: (Central Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park] , and  [LINK: (Grand Central Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Central_Terminal] . [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Ann_Shields-25]   [LINK: (New York Penn Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Penn_Station]  is the busiest transportation hub in the  [LINK: (Western Hemisphere) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Hemisphere] . [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-26]   [LINK: (Chinatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinatown,_Manhattan]  has the highest concentration of  [LINK: (Chinese people) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_people_in_New_York_City]  in the Western Hemisphere. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-27]   [LINK: (Fifth Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Avenue]  has been ranked as the most expensive shopping street in the world, before falling to second in 2024. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-28]  [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-FifthAvenueMostExpensiveStreetOnEarth-29]  The borough hosts many prominent  [LINK: (bridges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridges_and_tunnels_in_New_York_City] ,  [LINK: (tunnels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridges_and_tunnels_in_New_York_City] , and  [LINK: (skyscrapers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tallest_buildings_in_New_York_City]  including the  [LINK: (Empire State Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_State_Building] ,  [LINK: (Chrysler Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysler_Building] , and  [LINK: (One World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_World_Trade_Center] . [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-30]  It is also home to the  [LINK: (National Basketball Association) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Basketball_Association] 's  [LINK: (New York Knicks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Knicks]  and the  [LINK: (National Hockey League) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Hockey_League] 's  [LINK: (New York Rangers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Rangers] .


# HISTORY #

Manhattan was historically part of the  [LINK: (Lenapehoking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenapehoking]  territory inhabited by the  [LINK: (Munsee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munsee] ,  [LINK: (Lenape) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenape] , [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-31]  and  [LINK: (Wappinger) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wappinger]   [LINK: (tribes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_Americans_in_the_United_States] . [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-nb-32]  There were several Lenape settlements in the area including  [LINK: (Sapohanikan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapohanikan] ,  [LINK: (Nechtanc) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nechtanc] , and  [LINK: (Konaande Kongh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konaande_Kongh] , which were interconnected by a series of trails. The primary trail on the island, which would later become  [LINK: (Broadway) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_(Manhattan)] , ran from what is now  [LINK: (Inwood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inwood,_Manhattan]  in the north to  [LINK: (Battery Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battery_Park]  in the south. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-33]  There were various sites for  [LINK: (fishing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing]  and planting established by the Lenape throughout Manhattan. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-:1-15]

The  [LINK: (etymology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etymology]  of the name Manhattan is most likely — amongst other theories, and via  [LINK: (loaning) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loanword]  by  [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_language]  — from the  [LINK: (Lenape) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lenape] 's local language  [LINK: (Munsee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munsee_language] , manaháhtaan (where manah- means "gather", -aht- means " [LINK: (bow) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_(archery)] ", and -aan is used to form verb  [LINK: (stems) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_stem] ). The Lenape word has been translated as "the place where we get bows" or "place for gathering the (wood to make) bows". According to a Munsee tradition recorded by  [LINK: (Albert Seqaqkind Anthony) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Anthony]  in the 19th century, the island was named for a grove of  [LINK: (hickory trees) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hickory_tree]  that was considered ideal for bowmaking. An alternate theory claims a " [LINK: (Delaware) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_languages]  source akin to  [LINK: (Munsee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munsee]  munahan ("island")." [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-34]  [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-35]

In April 1524,  [LINK: (Florentine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florence]  explorer  [LINK: (Giovanni da Verrazzano) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_da_Verrazzano] , sailing in service of  [LINK: (Francis I of France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_I_of_France] , became the first documented European to visit the area that would become New York City. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-36]  Verrazzano entered the  [LINK: (tidal strait) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tidal_strait]  now known as  [LINK: (The Narrows) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Narrows]  and named the land around  [LINK: (Upper New York Harbor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_New_York_Harbor]   [LINK: (New Angoulême) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Angoul%C3%AAme] , in reference to the family name of  [LINK: (King Francis I) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_I_of_France] ; he sailed far enough into the harbor to sight the  [LINK: (Hudson River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River] , and he named the Bay of Santa Margarita – what is now  [LINK: (Upper New York Bay) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Harbor]  – after  [LINK: (Marguerite de Navarre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marguerite_de_Navarre] , the elder sister of the king. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-37]  [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-38]

Manhattan was first mapped during a 1609 voyage of  [LINK: (Henry Hudson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Hudson] . [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-39]  Hudson came across Manhattan Island and the native people living there, and continued up the river that would later bear his name, the  [LINK: (Hudson River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River] . [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-SciAm-40]  Manhattan was first recorded in writing as Manna-hata, in the logbook of Robert Juet, an officer on the voyage. [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-41]

A permanent European presence in  [LINK: (New Netherland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Netherland]  began in 1624, with the founding of a  [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Republic]   [LINK: (fur trading) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fur_trade]  settlement on  [LINK: (Governors Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governors_Island] . [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-42]  In 1625, construction was started on the  [LINK: (citadel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citadel]  of  [LINK: (Fort Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Amsterdam]  on Manhattan Island, later called  [LINK: (New Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Amsterdam]  (Nieuw Amsterdam), in what is now Lower Manhattan. [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-43]  [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Tolerance-44]  [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-45]  The establishment of Fort Amsterdam is recognized as the birth of New York City. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-46] 
In 1647,  [LINK: (Peter Stuyvesant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Stuyvesant]  was appointed as the last Dutch Director-General of the colony. [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-47]  New Amsterdam was formally incorporated as a city on February 2, 1653. [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-48]  In 1664, English forces conquered New Netherland and renamed it "New York" after the English  [LINK: (Duke of York and Albany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_II_of_England] , the future King James II. [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-49]  In August 1673, the  [LINK: (Dutch reconquered) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reconquest_of_New_Netherland]  the colony, renaming it "New Orange", but permanently relinquished it back to England the following year under the terms of the  [LINK: (Treaty of Westminster) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Westminster_(1674)]  that ended the  [LINK: (Third Anglo-Dutch War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Anglo-Dutch_War] . [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-50]  [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-51]

Manhattan was at the heart of the  [LINK: (New York Campaign) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_and_New_Jersey_campaign] , a series of major battles in the early stages of the  [LINK: (American Revolutionary War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Revolutionary_War] . The  [LINK: (Continental Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_Army]  was forced to abandon Manhattan after the  [LINK: (Battle of Fort Washington) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fort_Washington]  on November 16, 1776. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-53]  The city, greatly damaged by the  [LINK: (Great Fire of New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_New_York_(1776)]  during the campaign, became the British military and political center of operations in North America for the remainder of the war. [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-54]  British occupation lasted until November 25, 1783, when  [LINK: (George Washington) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Washington]  returned to Manhattan, a day celebrated as  [LINK: (Evacuation Day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evacuation_Day_(New_York)] , marking when the last British forces left the city. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-55]

From January 11, 1785, until 1789, New York City was the fifth of five  [LINK: (capitals of the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_capitals_in_the_United_States]  under the  [LINK: (Articles of Confederation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Articles_of_Confederation] , with the  [LINK: (Continental Congress) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_Congress]  meeting at  [LINK: (New York City Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Hall]  (then at  [LINK: (Fraunces Tavern) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fraunces_Tavern] ). [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-56]  New York was the first capital under the newly enacted  [LINK: (Constitution of the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Constitution] , from March 4, 1789, to August 12, 1790, at  [LINK: (Federal Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Hall] . [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-senate-17]  Federal Hall was where the  [LINK: (United States Supreme Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Supreme_Court]  met for the first time, [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-57]  the  [LINK: (United States Bill of Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Bill_of_Rights]  were drafted and ratified, [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-cwf-58]  and where the  [LINK: (Northwest Ordinance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northwest_Ordinance]  was adopted, establishing measures for  [LINK: (admission to the Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Admission_to_the_Union]  of new states. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-59]

New York grew as an economic center, first as a result of  [LINK: (Alexander Hamilton) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Hamilton] 's policies and practices as the first  [LINK: (Secretary of the Treasury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secretary_of_the_Treasury]  to expand the city's role as a center of commerce and industry. [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-60]  In 1810, New York City, then confined to Manhattan, had surpassed  [LINK: (Philadelphia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philadelphia]  as the most populous city in the United States. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-61]  The  [LINK: (Commissioners' Plan of 1811) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commissioners%27_Plan_of_1811]  laid out the island of Manhattan in its familiar  [LINK: (grid plan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grid_plan] . [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-MCNY1811-62]  The city's role as an economic center grew with the opening of the  [LINK: (Erie Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erie_Canal]  in 1825, cutting transportation costs by 90% compared to road transport and connecting the Atlantic port to the vast agricultural markets of the  [LINK: (Midwestern United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midwestern_United_States]  and  [LINK: (Canada) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada] . [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-63]  [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-lankevich-p67-64]  [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-65]

[LINK: (Tammany Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tammany_Hall] , a  [LINK: (Democratic Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)]   [LINK: (political machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_machine] , began to grow in influence with the support of many of the  [LINK: (immigrant Irish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_diaspora] , culminating in the election of the first Tammany mayor,  [LINK: (Fernando Wood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_Wood] , in 1854. [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-66]  Covering 840 acres (340 ha) in the center of the island,  [LINK: (Central Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park] , which opened its first portions to the public in 1858, became the first  [LINK: (landscaped public park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arboriculture]  in an American city. [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-67]  [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-68]  [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-69]  [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-70]

New York City played a complex role in the  [LINK: (American Civil War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Civil_War] . The city had strong commercial ties to the  [LINK: (South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_United_States] , but anger around  [LINK: (conscription) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conscription] , resentment against Lincoln's war policies and paranoia about  [LINK: (free Blacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_negro]  taking the jobs of poor immigrants [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-71]  culminated in the three-day-long  [LINK: (New York Draft Riots) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Draft_Riots]  of July 1863, among the worst incidents of  [LINK: (civil disorder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_disorder]  in American history. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-72]  The rate of immigration from Europe grew steeply after the Civil War, and Manhattan became the first stop for millions seeking a new life in the United States, a role acknowledged by the dedication of the  [LINK: (Statue of Liberty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statue_of_Liberty]  in 1886. [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-73]  [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-74]  This immigration brought further social upheaval. In a city of tenements packed with poorly paid laborers from dozens of nations, the city became a hotbed of  [LINK: (revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolution]  (including  [LINK: (anarchists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchist]  and  [LINK: (communists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communist]  among others),  [LINK: (syndicalism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syndicalism] ,  [LINK: (racketeering) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racketeering] , and  [LINK: (unionization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unionization] .[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

In 1883, the opening of the  [LINK: (Brooklyn Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklyn_Bridge]  across the  [LINK: (East River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_River]  established a road connection to  [LINK: (Brooklyn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklyn]  and the rest of  [LINK: (Long Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Island] . [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-75]  In 1898, New York City consolidated with three neighboring counties to form "the  [LINK: (City of Greater New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Greater_New_York] ", and Manhattan was established as one of the five  [LINK: (boroughs of New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boroughs_of_New_York_City] . [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-76]  [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-77]   [LINK: (The Bronx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bronx]  remained part of New York County until 1914, when Bronx County was established. [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-78]

The construction of the  [LINK: (New York City Subway) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Subway] , which opened in 1904, helped bind the new city together, [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-79]  as did the completion of the  [LINK: (Williamsburg Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williamsburg_Bridge]  (1903) and  [LINK: (Manhattan Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan_Bridge]  (1909) connecting to Brooklyn and the  [LINK: (Queensboro Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queensboro_Bridge]  (1909) connecting to Queens. [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-80]  In the 1920s, Manhattan experienced large arrivals of African-Americans as part of the  [LINK: (Great Migration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Migration_(African_American)]  from the southern United States, and the  [LINK: (Harlem Renaissance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_Renaissance] , [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-81]  part of a larger boom time in the  [LINK: (Prohibition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prohibition_in_the_United_States]  era that included new  [LINK: (skyscrapers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyscraper]  competing for the skyline, with the  [LINK: (Woolworth Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woolworth_Building]  (1913),  [LINK: (40 Wall Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/40_Wall_Street]  (1930), the  [LINK: (Chrysler Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysler_Building]  (1930), and the  [LINK: (Empire State Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_State_Building]  (1931) leapfrogging each other to take their place as the  [LINK: (world's tallest building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_world%27s_tallest_buildings] . [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-82]

Manhattan's majority  [LINK: (white) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_American]  ethnic group declined from 98.7% in 1900 to 58.3% by 1990. [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Census1790to1990-83]  On March 25, 1911, the  [LINK: (Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triangle_Shirtwaist_Factory_fire]  in  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village]  killed 146  [LINK: (garment workers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garment_worker] , [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-84]  leading to overhauls of the city's fire department,  [LINK: (building codes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Building_code] , and workplace safety regulations. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-85]  In 1912, about 20,000 workers, a quarter of them women, marched upon  [LINK: (Washington Square Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Square_Park]  to commemorate the fire. Many of the women wore fitted tucked-front blouses like those manufactured by the company, a clothing style that became the working woman's uniform and a symbol of  [LINK: (women's liberation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s_liberation] , reflecting the alliance of the labor and  [LINK: (suffrage) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suffrage]  movements. [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-86]

Despite the  [LINK: (Great Depression) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression] , some of the world's tallest skyscrapers were completed in Manhattan during the 1930s, including numerous  [LINK: (Art Deco) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Deco]  masterpieces that are still part of the city's skyline, most notably the Empire State Building, the Chrysler Building, and  [LINK: (30 Rockefeller Plaza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GE_Building] . [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-87]  A postwar economic boom led to the development of huge housing developments targeted at returning veterans, the largest being  [LINK: (Stuyvesant Town–Peter Cooper Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuyvesant_Town%E2%80%93Peter_Cooper_Village] , which opened in 1947. [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-88]  [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-89]  The  [LINK: (United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations]  relocated to a new  [LINK: (headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headquarters_of_the_United_Nations]  that was completed in 1952 along the East River. [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-90]  [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-91]  [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-92]

The  [LINK: (Stonewall riots) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_riots]  were a series of spontaneous, violent protests by members of the  [LINK: (gay community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBT_community]  against a  [LINK: (police raid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_raid]  that took place in the early morning hours of June 28, 1969, at the  [LINK: (Stonewall Inn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_Inn]  in the  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village]  neighborhood of Lower Manhattan. They are widely considered to constitute the single most important event leading to the  [LINK: (gay liberation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gay_liberation]  movement [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-GayGreenwichVillage1-93]  [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-KentuckyStonewall-94]  and the modern fight for  [LINK: (LGBT rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBT_rights_by_country_or_territory] . [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NPSStonewall-95]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ObamaStonewall-21]  [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-GayborhoodGreenwichVillage-96]  [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NPS99000562-20]  [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-97]

In the 1970s, job losses due to  [LINK: (industrial restructuring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deindustrialization]  caused New York City, including Manhattan, to suffer from economic problems and rising crime rates. [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-98]  While a resurgence in the  [LINK: (financial industry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_industry]  greatly improved the city's economic health in the 1980s, New York's crime rate continued to increase through the decade and into the beginning of the 1990s. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-99]  The 1980s saw a rebirth of  [LINK: (Wall Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall_Street] , and Manhattan reclaimed its role as the world's  [LINK: (financial center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_center] , with Wall Street employment doubling from 1977 to 1987. [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-100]  The 1980s also saw Manhattan at the heart of the  [LINK: (AIDS crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIDS_crisis] , with Greenwich Village at its epicenter. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-101]

In the 1970s,  [LINK: (Times Square) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Times_Square]  and  [LINK: (42nd Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/42nd_Street_(Manhattan)]  – with its  [LINK: (sex shops) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sex_shop] ,  [LINK: (peep shows) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peep_show] , and adult theaters, along with its  [LINK: (sex trade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prostitution] , street crime, and public drug use – became emblematic of the city's decline, with a 1981 article in  [LINK: (Rolling Stone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_Stone]  magazine calling the stretch of West 42nd Street between  [LINK: (7th) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7th_Avenue_(Manhattan)]  and  [LINK: (8th Avenues) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/8th_Avenue_(Manhattan)]  the "sleaziest block in America". [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-102]  By the late 1990s, led by efforts by the city and the  [LINK: (Walt Disney Company) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walt_Disney_Company] , the area had been revived as a center of tourism to the point where it was described by  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times]  as "arguably the most sought-after 13 acres of commercial property in the world." [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-103]

By the 1990s, crime rates began to drop dramatically [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-104]  [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-105]  and the city once again became the destination of immigrants from around the world, joining with low  [LINK: (interest rates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interest_rate]  and Wall Street  [LINK: (bonus payments) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonus_payment]  to fuel the growth of the real estate market. [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-106]  Important new sectors, such as  [LINK: (Silicon Alley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_Alley] , emerged in the  [LINK: (Flatiron District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatiron_District] , cementing technology as a key component of Manhattan's economy. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-107]

The  [LINK: (1993 World Trade Center bombing) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1993_World_Trade_Center_bombing] , described by the  [LINK: (FBI) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Bureau_of_Investigation]  as "something of a deadly dress rehearsal for 9/11", was a terrorist attack in which six people were killed when a van bomb filled with explosives was detonated in a parking lot below the  [LINK: (North Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tenants_in_1_World_Trade_Center_(1971%E2%80%932001)]  of the  [LINK: (World Trade Center complex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(1973%E2%80%932001)] . [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-108]

On September 11, 2001, the  [LINK: (Twin Towers of the original World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(1973%E2%80%932001)]  were struck by hijacked aircraft and collapsed in the  [LINK: (September 11 attacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_attacks]  launched by  [LINK: (al-Qaeda) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Qaeda]  terrorists. The collapse caused extensive damage to surrounding buildings and skyscrapers in Lower Manhattan, and resulted in the  [LINK: (deaths of 2,606) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casualties_of_the_September_11_attacks]  of the 17,400 who had been in the buildings when the planes hit, in addition to those on the planes. [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-109]  Since 2001, most of  [LINK: (Lower Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Manhattan]  has been restored, although  [LINK: (there has been controversy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_rebuilding_controversy]  surrounding the rebuilding. In 2014, the new  [LINK: (One World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_World_Trade_Center] , at 1,776 feet (541 m) measured to the top of its spire, became the tallest building in the Western Hemisphere [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-110]  and is the  [LINK: (world's seventh-tallest building (as of 2023)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings] . [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-111]

The  [LINK: (Occupy Wall Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupy_Wall_Street]  protests in  [LINK: (Zuccotti Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zuccotti_Park]  in the  [LINK: (Financial District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_District_(Manhattan)]  of Lower Manhattan began on September 17, 2011, receiving global attention and spawning the  [LINK: (Occupy movement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupy_movement]  against  [LINK: (social) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_inequality]  and  [LINK: (economic inequality) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_inequality]  worldwide. [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-112]  [LINK: ([111]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-113]

On October 29 and 30, 2012,  [LINK: (Hurricane Sandy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Sandy]  caused  [LINK: (extensive destruction) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effects_of_Hurricane_Sandy_in_New_York]  in the borough, ravaging portions of Lower Manhattan with record-high  [LINK: (storm surge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storm_surge]  from New York Harbor, [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NYC_after_Sandy-114]  severe flooding, and high winds, causing  [LINK: (power outages) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_outage]  for hundreds of thousands of city residents [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-115]  and leading to  [LINK: (gasoline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasoline]  shortages [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-116]  and disruption of  [LINK: (mass transit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_transit]  systems. [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-117]  [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-118]  [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-119]  [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-120]  The storm and its profound impacts have prompted discussion of constructing  [LINK: (seawalls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seawall]  and other  [LINK: (coastal barriers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coastal_management]  around the  [LINK: (shorelines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoreline]  of the borough and the metropolitan area to minimize the risk of destructive consequences from another such event in the future. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-121]


# GEOGRAPHY #

According to the  [LINK: (United States Census Bureau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Census_Bureau] , New York County has a total area of 33.6 square miles (87 km2), of which 22.8 square miles (59 km2) is land and 10.8 square miles (28 km2) (32%) is water. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-CensusArea-2]  The northern segment of Upper Manhattan represents a geographic  [LINK: (panhandle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salient_(geography)] . Manhattan Island is 22.7 square miles (59 km2) in area, 13.4 miles (21.6 km) long and 2.3 miles (3.7 km) wide, at its widest point, near  [LINK: (14th Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14th_Street_(Manhattan)] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Passikoff-4]  [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-122]

The borough consists primarily of Manhattan Island, along with the  [LINK: (Marble Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Hill,_Manhattan]  neighborhood and several small islands, including  [LINK: (Randalls Island and Wards Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands]  and  [LINK: (Roosevelt Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roosevelt_Island]  in the East River; and  [LINK: (Governors Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governors_Island]  and  [LINK: (Liberty Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Island]  to the south in  [LINK: (New York Harbor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Harbor] . [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Islands-123]

The Island is about 13.4 miles (21.6 km) from north to south, and at its widest, 2.3 miles (3.7 km). [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Passikoff-4]  Manhattan Island is loosely divided into Downtown ( [LINK: (Lower Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Manhattan] ), Midtown ( [LINK: (Midtown Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midtown_Manhattan] ), and Uptown ( [LINK: (Upper Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_Manhattan] ), with  [LINK: (Fifth Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Avenue]  dividing Manhattan lengthwise into its  [LINK: (East Side) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Side_(Manhattan)]  and  [LINK: (West Side) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Side_(Manhattan)] . [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-124]  Manhattan Island is bounded by the  [LINK: (Hudson River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River]  to the west and the  [LINK: (East River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_River]  to the east. To the north, the  [LINK: (Harlem River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_River]  divides Manhattan Island from  [LINK: (the Bronx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bronx]  and the mainland United States. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-125]

Early in the 19th century,  [LINK: (land reclamation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_reclamation]  was used to expand Lower Manhattan from the natural Hudson shoreline at  [LINK: (Greenwich Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Street]  to  [LINK: (West Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Street_(Manhattan)] . [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-126]  When  [LINK: (building the World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Construction_of_the_World_Trade_Center]  in 1968, 1.2 million cubic yards (920,000 m3) of material excavated from the site [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-127]  was used to expand the Manhattan  [LINK: (shoreline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoreline]  across West Street, creating  [LINK: (Battery Park City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battery_Park_City] . [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-128]  Constructed on piers at a cost of $260 million,  [LINK: (Little Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Island_at_Pier_55]  opened on the Hudson River in May 2021, connected to the western termini of  [LINK: (13th) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/13th_Street_(Manhattan)]  and  [LINK: (14th Streets) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14th_Street_(Manhattan)]  by  [LINK: (footbridges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Footbridge] . [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-129]

Marble Hill was part of the northern tip of Manhattan Island, but the  [LINK: (Harlem River Ship Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_River_Ship_Canal] , dug in 1895 to better connect the  [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem_River]  and  [LINK: (Hudson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River]  rivers, separated it from the remainder of Manhattan. [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-canal-130]  Before World War I, the section of the original Harlem River channel separating Marble Hill from the Bronx was filled in, and Marble Hill became part of the mainland. [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-131]  After a May 1984 court ruling that Marble Hill was simultaneously part of the Borough of Manhattan (not the Borough of the Bronx) and part of Bronx County (not New York County), [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-132]  the matter was definitively settled later that year when the  [LINK: (New York Legislature) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Legislature]  overwhelmingly passed legislation declaring the neighborhood part of both New York County and the Borough of Manhattan. [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-133]  [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-134]

Within  [LINK: (New York Harbor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Harbor] , there are three smaller islands:

[LINK: (Ellis Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellis_Island] , shared with  [LINK: (New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Jersey]  [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-135]

[LINK: (Governors Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governors_Island]  [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-136]

[LINK: (Liberty Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Island]  (administered by the  [LINK: (National Park Service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Park_Service] ) [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-137]

Other smaller islands, in the  [LINK: (East River) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_River] , include (from north to south):

[LINK: (Randalls and Wards Islands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands] , joined by landfill

[LINK: (Mill Rock) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mill_Rock]

[LINK: (Roosevelt Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roosevelt_Island] , which has a population of 14,000, extends for 2 miles (3.2 km), and was renamed in 1973 from Welfare Island to honor President  [LINK: (Franklin D. Roosevelt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_D._Roosevelt] . [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-138]

[LINK: (U Thant Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U_Thant_Island]  (legally Belmont Island)

The  [LINK: (bedrock) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedrock]  underlying much of Manhattan consists of three rock formations: Inwood  [LINK: (marble) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble] , Fordham  [LINK: (gneiss) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gneiss] , and Manhattan  [LINK: (schist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schist] , and is well suited for the foundations of Manhattan's  [LINK: (skyscrapers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyscrapers] . [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-139]  It is part of the  [LINK: (Manhattan Prong) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan_Prong]  physiographic region.

Under the  [LINK: (Köppen climate classification) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C3%B6ppen_climate_classification] , New York City features both a  [LINK: (humid subtropical climate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humid_subtropical_climate]  (Cfa) and a  [LINK: (humid continental climate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humid_continental_climate]  (Dfa). [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-140]  It is the northernmost major city on the North American continent with a humid subtropical climate. The city averages 234 days with at least some sunshine annually. [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-noaasun-141]

Winters are cold and damp, and prevailing wind patterns that blow offshore temper the moderating effects of the  [LINK: (Atlantic Ocean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Ocean] . The Atlantic and the partial shielding from colder air by the  [LINK: (Appalachians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appalachian_Mountains]  keep the city warmer in the winter than inland North American cities at similar or lesser latitudes. The daily mean temperature in January, the area's coldest month, is 32.6 °F (0.3 °C). [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA_txt-142]  Temperatures usually drop to 10 °F (−12 °C) several times per winter, [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA_txt-142]  [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NYC_climate-143]  and reach 60 °F (16 °C) several days in the coldest winter month. [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA_txt-142]  Spring and autumn are unpredictable and can range from chilly to warm, although they are usually mild with low humidity. Summers are typically warm to hot and humid, with a daily mean temperature of 76.5 °F (24.7 °C) in July. [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA_txt-142]

Nighttime conditions are often exacerbated by the  [LINK: (urban heat island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_heat_island]  phenomenon, which causes heat absorbed during the day to be radiated back at night, raising temperatures by as much as 7 °F (4 °C) when winds are slow. [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-144]  Daytime temperatures exceed 90 °F (32 °C) on average of 17 days each summer [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA-145]  and in some years exceed 100 °F (38 °C). Extreme temperatures have ranged from −15 °F (−26 °C), recorded on February 9, 1934, up to 106 °F (41 °C) on July 9, 1936. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA-145]  Manhattan lies in USDA  [LINK: (plant hardiness zone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardiness_zone]  7b (5 to 10 °F/-15 to -12.2 °C). [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-146]

Manhattan receives 49.9 inches (1,270 mm) of  [LINK: (precipitation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precipitation]  annually, which is relatively evenly spread throughout the year. Average winter snowfall between 1981 and 2010 has been 25.8 inches (66 cm). This varies considerably from year to year. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-New_York_City_Weatherbox_NOAA-145]

[LINK: (v) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:New_York_City_weatherbox]

[LINK: (t) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:New_York_City_weatherbox]

[LINK: (e) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:New_York_City_weatherbox]

Manhattan's many neighborhoods are not named according to any particular convention, nor do they have official boundaries. Some are geographical (the  [LINK: (Upper East Side) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_East_Side] ), or ethnically descriptive ( [LINK: (Little Italy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Italy,_Manhattan] ). Others are  [LINK: (acronyms) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acronyms] , such as  [LINK: (TriBeCa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TriBeCa]  (for "TRIangle BElow CAnal Street") or  [LINK: (SoHo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SoHo]  ("SOuth of HOuston"),  [LINK: (NoLIta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NoLIta,_Manhattan]  ("NOrth of Little ITAly"), and  [LINK: (NoMad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NoMad]  ("NOrth of MADison Square Park"). [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-151]  [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-152]  [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-153]  [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-154]  [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-155]   [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem]  is a name from the Dutch colonial era after  [LINK: (Haarlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haarlem] , a city in the Netherlands. [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-156]  Some have simple  [LINK: (folkloric) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folkloric]  names, such as  [LINK: (Hell's Kitchen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell%27s_Kitchen,_Manhattan] , alongside their more official but lesser used title (in this case, Clinton). [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-157]

Some neighborhoods, such as  [LINK: (SoHo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SoHo] , which is mixed use, are known for  [LINK: (upscale shopping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxury_goods]  as well as residential use. [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-158]  Others, such as  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village] , the  [LINK: (Lower East Side) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_East_Side,_Manhattan] ,  [LINK: (Alphabet City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphabet_City,_Manhattan]  and the  [LINK: (East Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Village,_Manhattan] , have long been associated with the  [LINK: (Bohemian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bohemianism]  subculture. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-159]  [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-160]  [LINK: ([157]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-161]   [LINK: (Chelsea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chelsea,_Manhattan]  is one of several Manhattan neighborhoods with large  [LINK: (gay populations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBT_culture_in_New_York_City]  and has become a center of both the international  [LINK: (art industry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_art]  and New York's nightlife. [LINK: ([158]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-162]   [LINK: (Chinatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinatown,_Manhattan]  has the highest concentration of people of  [LINK: (Chinese) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Americans_in_New_York_City]  descent outside of  [LINK: (Asia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asia] . [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-163]  [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-164]

[LINK: (Koreatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koreatown,_Manhattan]  is roughly centered on 32nd Street between Fifth and Sixth Avenues. [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-KoreatownExpanding-165]   [LINK: (Rose Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_Hill,_Manhattan]  features a growing number of Indian restaurants and spice shops along a stretch of  [LINK: (Lexington Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexington_Avenue]  between 25th and 30th Streets which has become known as  [LINK: (Curry Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_Hill] . [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-166]   [LINK: (Washington Heights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Heights,_Manhattan]  in  [LINK: (Uptown Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uptown_Manhattan]  is home to the largest  [LINK: (Dominican immigrant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominican_Americans#New_York_City]  community in the United States. [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-167]   [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem] , also in Upper Manhattan, is the historical epicenter of  [LINK: (African American) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_American]  culture. [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-168]  Since 2010, a  [LINK: (Little Australia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Australia]  has emerged and is growing in Nolita, Lower Manhattan. [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-LittleAustraliaNYC-169]

Manhattan has two central business districts, the  [LINK: (Financial District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_District,_Manhattan]  at the southern tip of the island, and  [LINK: (Midtown Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midtown_Manhattan] . The term uptown also refers to the northern part of Manhattan above  [LINK: (72nd Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/72nd_Street_(Manhattan)]  and downtown to the southern portion below  [LINK: (14th Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14th_Street_(Manhattan)] , [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NYCBasics-170]  with Midtown covering the area in between, though definitions can be fluid.  [LINK: (Fifth Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Avenue]  roughly bisects Manhattan Island and acts as the demarcation line for east/west designations. [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-NYCBasics-170]  [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-171]  South of  [LINK: (Waverly Place) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waverly_Place] , Fifth Avenue terminates and Broadway becomes the east/west demarcation line.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ] In Manhattan, uptown means north and downtown means south. [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ManhattanUptownDowntown-172]  This usage differs from that of most American cities, where downtown refers to the central business district.

