# DEMOGRAPHICS #

As of the  [LINK: (2020 census) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_census] , Manhattan's population had increased by 6.8% over the decade to 1,694,251, representing 19.2% of New York City's population of 8,804,190 and 8.4% of New York State's population of 20,201,249. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-QuickFacts-5]  The population density of New York County was 70,450.8 inhabitants per square mile (27,201.2/km2) in 2022, the highest population density of any county in the United States and  [LINK: (higher than the density of any individual U.S. city) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_United_States_cities_by_population_density#New_York_City_boroughs] . [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-173]  [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-174]  At the  [LINK: (2010 census) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_United_States_census] , there were 1,585,873 people living in Manhattan, an increase of 3.2% from the 1,537,195 counted in the  [LINK: (2000 census) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2000_United_States_census] . [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-175]

In 2010, the largest organized religious group in Manhattan was the  [LINK: (Archdiocese of New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Catholic_Archdiocese_of_New_York] , with 323,325 Catholics worshiping at 109 parishes, followed by 64,000  [LINK: (Orthodox) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthodox_Judaism]  Jews with 77 congregations, an estimated 42,545  [LINK: (Muslims) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam_in_the_United_States]  with 21 congregations, 42,502  [LINK: (non-denominational) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondenominational_Christianity]  adherents with 54 congregations, 26,178  [LINK: (TEC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Episcopal_Church_(United_States)]  Episcopalians with 46 congregations, 25,048  [LINK: (ABC-USA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Baptist_Churches_USA]  Baptists with 41 congregations, 24,536  [LINK: (Reform) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_Judaism]  Jews with 10 congregations, 23,982  [LINK: (Mahayana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahayana]  Buddhists with 35 congregations, 10,503  [LINK: (PC-USA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presbyterian_Church_in_the_United_States_of_America]  Presbyterians with 30 congregations, and 10,268  [LINK: (RCA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reformed_Church_in_America]  Presbyterians with 10 congregations. Altogether, 44.0% of the population was claimed as members by religious congregations, although members of historically African-American denominations were underrepresented due to incomplete information. [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-180]  In 2014, Manhattan had 703 religious organizations, the seventeenth most out of all US counties. [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-181]  There is a large  [LINK: (Buddhist temple) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddhist_temple]  in Manhattan located at the foot of the  [LINK: (Manhattan Bridge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan_Bridge]  in  [LINK: (Chinatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinatown,_Manhattan] . [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-182]

As of 2015, 60.0% (927,650) of Manhattan residents, aged five and older, spoke only English at home, while 22.63% (350,112) spoke Spanish, 5.37% (83,013) Chinese, 2.21% (34,246) French, 0.85% (13,138) Korean, 0.72% (11,135) Russian, and 0.70% (10,766) Japanese. In total, 40.0% of Manhattan's population, aged five and older, spoke a language other than English at home. [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-183]


# LANDMARKS AND ARCHITECTURE #

Points of interest on Manhattan Island include the  [LINK: (American Museum of Natural History) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Museum_of_Natural_History] ; the  [LINK: (Battery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Battery_(Manhattan)] ;  [LINK: (Broadway) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_(Manhattan)]  and the  [LINK: (Theater District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theater_District,_Manhattan] ;  [LINK: (Bryant Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bryant_Park] ;  [LINK: (Central Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park] ,  [LINK: (Chinatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinatown,_Manhattan] ; the  [LINK: (Chrysler Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysler_Building] ;  [LINK: (The Cloisters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cloisters] ;  [LINK: (Columbia University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University] ;  [LINK: (Curry Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_Hill] ; the  [LINK: (Empire State Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_State_Building] ;  [LINK: (Flatiron Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatiron_Building] ; the  [LINK: (Financial District) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_District,_Manhattan]  (including the  [LINK: (New York Stock Exchange Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Stock_Exchange_Building] ;  [LINK: (Wall Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall_Street] ; and the  [LINK: (South Street Seaport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Street_Seaport] );  [LINK: (Grand Central Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Central_Terminal] ;  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village]  (including  [LINK: (New York University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_University] ;  [LINK: (Washington Square Arch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Square_Arch] ; and  [LINK: (Stonewall Inn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonewall_Inn] );  [LINK: (Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harlem]  and  [LINK: (Spanish Harlem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_Harlem] ; the  [LINK: (High Line) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Line] ;  [LINK: (Koreatown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koreatown,_Manhattan] ;  [LINK: (Lincoln Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Center] ;  [LINK: (Little Australia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Australia,_Manhattan] ;  [LINK: (Little Italy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Italy,_Manhattan] ;  [LINK: (Madison Square Garden) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison_Square_Garden] ;  [LINK: (Museum Mile on Fifth Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Avenue#Museum_Mile]  (including the  [LINK: (Metropolitan Museum of Art) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art] );  [LINK: (New York Penn Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Penn_Station] ,  [LINK: (Port Authority Bus Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Authority_Bus_Terminal] ;  [LINK: (Rockefeller Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockefeller_Center]  (including  [LINK: (Radio City Music Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_City_Music_Hall] );  [LINK: (Times Square) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Times_Square] ; and the  [LINK: (World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(2001%E2%80%93present)]  (including the  [LINK: (National September 11 Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_September_11_Museum]  and  [LINK: (One World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_World_Trade_Center] ).

There are also numerous iconic  [LINK: (bridges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridges_and_tunnels_in_New_York_City]  across rivers that connect to Manhattan Island, as well as an emerging number of  [LINK: (supertall skyscrapers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_New_York_City] . The  [LINK: (Statue of Liberty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statue_of_Liberty]  rests on  [LINK: (Liberty Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Island] , an  [LINK: (exclave) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exclave]  of Manhattan, and part of  [LINK: (Ellis Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellis_Island]  is also an exclave of Manhattan. The borough has many  [LINK: (energy-efficient) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efficient_energy_use]  office buildings, such as the  [LINK: (Hearst Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hearst_Tower_(New_York_City)] , the rebuilt  [LINK: (7 World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7_World_Trade_Center] , [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-greenbuilding-184]  and the  [LINK: (Bank of America Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_of_America_Tower_(Manhattan)] —the first skyscraper designed to attain a Platinum  [LINK: (LEED) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leadership_in_Energy_and_Environmental_Design]  Certification. [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-185]  [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-186]

The  [LINK: (skyscraper) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyscraper] , which has shaped Manhattan's distinctive  [LINK: (skyline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyline] , has been closely associated with New York City's identity since the end of the 19th century. [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-187]  Structures such as the  [LINK: (Equitable Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equitable_Building_(Manhattan)]  of 1915, which rises vertically forty stories from the sidewalk, prompted the passage of the  [LINK: (1916 Zoning Resolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1916_Zoning_Resolution] , requiring new buildings to contain  [LINK: (setbacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Setback_(architecture)]  withdrawing progressively at a defined angle from the street as they rose, in order to preserve a view of the sky at street level. [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-nyt20160726-188]  Manhattan's skyline includes several buildings that are symbolic of New York, in particular the  [LINK: (Chrysler Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysler_Building]  [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-Willis_Friedman-189] : 14  and the  [LINK: (Empire State Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_State_Building] , which sees about 4 million visitors a year. [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-nyt20111225-190]

In 1961, the struggling  [LINK: (Pennsylvania Railroad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennsylvania_Railroad]  unveiled plans to tear down the old  [LINK: (Penn Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennsylvania_Station_(1910%E2%80%931963)]  and replace it with a new  [LINK: (Madison Square Garden) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison_Square_Garden]  and  [LINK: (office building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Penn_Plaza]  complex. [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-191]  Organized protests were aimed at preserving the  [LINK: (McKim, Mead & White) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McKim,_Mead_%26_White] -designed structure completed in 1910, widely considered a masterpiece of the  [LINK: (Beaux-Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaux-Arts_architecture]  style and one of the architectural jewels of New York City. [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-192]  Despite these efforts, demolition of the structure began in October 1963. [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-193]  The loss of Penn Station led directly to the enactment in 1965 of a local law establishing the  [LINK: (New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Landmarks_Preservation_Commission] , which is responsible for preserving the "city's historic, aesthetic, and  [LINK: (cultural heritage) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_heritage] ". [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-194]  The  [LINK: (historic preservation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historic_preservation]  movement triggered by Penn Station's demise has been credited with the retention of some one million structures nationwide, including over 1,000 in New York City. [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-195]  In 2017, a multibillion-dollar rebuilding plan was unveiled to restore the historic grandeur of Penn Station, in the process of upgrading the landmark's status as a critical  [LINK: (transportation hub) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transportation_hub] . [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-196]

The 700,000 ft2 (65,000 m2)  [LINK: (Moynihan Train Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moynihan_Train_Hall] , developed as a $1.6 billion renovation and expansion of Penn Station into the  [LINK: (James A. Farley Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_A._Farley_Building] , the city's former main post office building, was opened in January 2021. [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-197]

