[LINK: (Theodore Roosevelt Birthplace National Historic Site) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Roosevelt_Birthplace_National_Historic_Site]

[LINK: (Parkland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park]  covers a total of 2,659 acres (10.76 km2), accounting for 18.2% of the borough's land area; the 840-acre (3.4 km2)  [LINK: (Central Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park]  is the borough's largest park, comprising 31.6% of Manhattan's parkland. [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ParkList-198]  Designed by  [LINK: (Frederick Law Olmsted) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_Law_Olmsted]  and  [LINK: (Calvert Vaux) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calvert_Vaux] , the park is anchored by the 12-acre (4.9 ha)  [LINK: (Great Lawn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Lawn_and_Turtle_Pond]  [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-199]  and offers extensive  [LINK: (walking tracks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walking_track] , two  [LINK: (ice-skating) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice-skating]  rinks, a wildlife sanctuary, and several lawns and sporting areas, as well as 21 playgrounds, [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-200]  and a 6-mile (9.7 km) road from which automobile traffic has been banned since 2018. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-201]  While much of the park looks natural, it is almost entirely landscaped; the  [LINK: (construction of Central Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Construction_of_Central_Park]  in the 1850s was one of the era's most massive public works projects, with some 20,000 workers moving 5 million cubic yards (3.8 million cubic meters) of material to shape the  [LINK: (topography) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topography]  and create the English-style pastoral landscape that Olmsted and Vaux sought. [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-202]

The remaining 70% of Manhattan's parkland includes 204 playgrounds, 251 Greenstreets, 371  [LINK: (basketball courts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basketball_court] , and many other amenities. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-MBPOEnvironment-203]  The next-largest park in Manhattan, the  [LINK: (Hudson River Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hudson_River_Park] , stretches 4.5 miles (7.2 km) along the Hudson River and comprises 550 acres (220 ha). [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-RIoG_ParkStats-204]  Other major parks include: [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_note-ParkList-198]

