[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-72]   [LINK: (Ward, Geoffrey C.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoffrey_C._Ward]  "Gangs of New York" Archived July 16, 2019, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] , a review of  [LINK: (Paradise Alley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Alley]  by  [LINK: (Kevin Baker) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Baker_(author)] ,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , October 6, 2002. Accessed June 30, 2009. "The New York draft riots remain the worst civil disturbance in American history: according to the historian Adrian Cook, 119 people are known to have been killed, mostly rioters or onlookers who got too close when federal troops, brought back from the battlefield to restore order, started shooting."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-73]  Statue of Liberty Archived March 16, 2016, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] ,  [LINK: (National Park Service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Park_Service] . Accessed May 17, 2007.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-74]  "New Jerseyans' Claim To Liberty I. Rejected" Archived March 28, 2019, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] ,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , October 6, 1987. Accessed June 30, 2009. "The Supreme Court today refused to strip the Statue of Liberty of its status as a New Yorker. The Court, without comment, turned away a move by a two New Jerseyans to claim jurisdiction over the landmark for their state."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-75]  Brooklyn Bridge,  [LINK: (New York City Department of Transportation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Department_of_Transportation] . Accessed November 30, 2023. "The Brooklyn Bridge was designed by John A. Roebling. Construction began in 1869 and was completed in 1883.... The Brooklyn Bridge connects the boroughs of Manhattan and Brooklyn by spanning the East River."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-76]  Consolidation of the Five-Borough City: 1898,  [LINK: (New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City_Landmarks_Preservation_Commission] . Accessed November 30, 2023. "On January 1, 1898, the separate jurisdictions of New York (Manhattan), Brooklyn, the Bronx, Queens, and Staten Island joined together to form a single metropolis: the City of Greater New York. Movements for consolidation had been considered as far back as 1820, but by the end of the 19th century proponents were claiming that a single metropolitan jurisdiction stretching over five boroughs would run more efficiently and cement New York as the economic and cultural capital of the nation."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-77]  McFadden, Robert D. "Rockets' Red Glare Marked Birth of Merged City in 1898",  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , January 1, 1973. Accessed November 30, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-78]  "Birth of a Borough", A Walk Through the Bronx. Accessed January 3, 2024. "After consolidation in 1898, the twenty-third and twenty-fourth wards became the borough of the Bronx, which with Manhattan remained part of New York County (the other boroughs were already separate counties).... It was not until 1912, however, that the state legislature established the County of the Bronx as the sixty-second county in the state, effective January 1, 1914."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-79]  Opening ceremonies, New York subway, Oct. 27, 1904,  [LINK: (Library of Congress) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Congress] . Accessed December 1, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-80]  Dim, Joan Marans. "New York's Golden Age of Bridges",  [LINK: (Fordham University Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fordham_University_Press] , 2012.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-0-8232-5308-1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-8232-5308-1] . Accessed December 4, 2023. "The Williamsburg followed in 1903, the Queensboro (renamed the Ed Koch Queensboro Bridge) and the Manhattan in 1909, the George Washington in 1931, the Triborough (renamed the Robert F. Kennedy Bridge) in 1936, the Bronx-Whitestone in 1939, the Throgs Neck in 1961, and the Verrazano-Narrows in 1964."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-81]  A New African American Identity: The Harlem Renaissance,  [LINK: (National Museum of African American History and Culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Museum_of_African_American_History_and_Culture] . Accessed December 1, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-82]  Barr, Jason M. "Why Doesn't New York Construct the World's Tallest Building Anymore?", Building the Skyline, December 23, 2020. Accessed December 4, 2023. "Generation II was the twentieth century before World War I. This crop included the Singer Building (1908, 674 feet, 205 meters, 41 stories), the Metropolitan Life Tower (1909, 700 feet, 210 meters, 50 stories), and the Woolworth Building (1913, 792 feet, 241 meters, 55 stories).... Left to Right: Bank of Manhattan Building (1930), Chrysler Building (1930), Empire State Building (1931)."

^  [LINK: (a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-Census1790to1990_83-0]   [LINK: (b) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-Census1790to1990_83-1]   [LINK: (c) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-Census1790to1990_83-2]   [LINK: (d) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-Census1790to1990_83-3]   [LINK: (e) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-Census1790to1990_83-4]  Gibson, Campbell; and Jung, Kay. Historical Census Statistics On Population Totals By Race, 1790 to 1990, and By Hispanic Origin, 1970 to 1990, For Large Cities And Other Urban Places In The United States,  [LINK: (United States Census Bureau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Census_Bureau] , February 2005. Accessed December 27, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-84]  The Triangle Shirtwaist Factory Fire,  [LINK: (Occupational Safety and Health Administration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_Safety_and_Health_Administration] . Accessed December 1, 2023. "One hundred years ago on March 25, fire spread through the cramped Triangle Waist Company garment factory on the 8th, 9th and 10th floors of the Asch Building in lower Manhattan. Workers in the factory, many of whom were young women recently arrived from Europe, had little time or opportunity to escape. The rapidly spreading fire killed 146 workers."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-85]  Markel, Howard. "How the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire transformed labor laws and protected workers' health",  [LINK: (PBS NewsHour) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PBS_NewsHour] , March 31, 2021. Accessed December 4, 2023. "Activists kept their memory alive by lobbying their local and state leaders to do something in the name of building and worker safety and health. Three months later, John Alden Dix, then the governor of New York, signed a law empowering the Factory Investigating Committee, which resulted in eight more laws covering fire safety, factory inspection, and sanitation and employment rules for women and children. The following year, 1912, activists and legislators in New York State enacted another 25 laws that transformed its labor protections among the most progressive in the nation."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-86]  The Triangle Factory Fire Archived May 12, 2012, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] ,  [LINK: (Cornell University School of Industrial and Labor Relations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornell_University_School_of_Industrial_and_Labor_Relations] . Accessed April 25, 2007.

