[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-158]  Joe Lovinger (July 27, 2021). "The shopping district that banned retail: Soho's incoherent zoning". The Real Deal.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-159]  "Bohemia in Greenwich Village". Library of Congress. Retrieved September 13, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-160]  Hannah Fons (August 2009). "A Look at Manhattan's East Village". Cooperator News.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-161]   [LINK: (Bruni, Frank) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Bruni] . "The Grounds He Stamped: The New York Of Ginsberg" Archived March 28, 2019, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] ,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , April 7, 1997. Accessed June 30, 2009. "Indeed, for all the worldwide attention that Mr. Ginsberg received, he was always a creature and icon principally of downtown Manhattan, his world view forged in its crucible of political and sexual passions, his eccentricities nurtured by those of its peculiar demimonde, his individual myth entwined with that of the bohemian East Village in which he made his home. He embodied the East Village and the Lower East Side, Bill Morgan, a friend and Mr. Ginsberg's archivist, said yesterday."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-162]  Dunlap, David W. "The New Chelsea's Many Faces" Archived March 28, 2019, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] , The New York Times, November 13, 1994. Accessed June 30, 2009. "Gay Chelsea's role has solidified with the arrival of A Different Light bookstore, a cultural cornerstone that had been housed for a decade in an 800-square-foot (74 m2) nook at 548 Hudson Street, near Perry Street. It now takes up more than 5,000 square feet (500 m2) at 151 West 19th Street and its migration seems to embody a northward shift of gay life from Greenwich Village... Because of Chelsea's reputation, Mr. Garmendia said, single women were not likely to move in. But single men did. "The whole neighborhood became gay during the 70's", he said."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-163]  Grimes, Christopher. "World News: New York's Chinatown starts to feel the pinch over 'the bug'",  [LINK: (Financial Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_Times] , April 14, 2003. Accessed May 19, 2007. "New York's Chinatown is the site of the largest concentration of Chinese people in the western hemisphere."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-164]  "Chinatown: A World of Dining, Shopping, and History". Archived from the original on July 9, 2006. Retrieved April 27, 2007.,  [LINK: (NYC & Company) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NYC_%26_Company] . Accessed June 30, 2009. "No visit to New York City is complete without exploring the sights, cuisines, history, and shops of the biggest Chinatown in the United States. The largest concentration of Chinese people—150,000—in the Western Hemisphere are in a two-square-mile area in downtown Manhattan that's loosely bounded by Lafayette, Worth, and Grand streets and East Broadway."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-KoreatownExpanding_165-0]  Gina Pace (April 26, 2015). "Koreatown in NYC is now being taken more seriously as a dining destination". New York Daily News. Archived from the original on October 11, 2016.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-166]  Ensminger, Kris. "More Than Tandoori" Archived September 1, 2017, at the  [LINK: (Wayback Machine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine] ,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , November 20, 2016. "Curry Hill, centered on Lexington Avenue and 28th Street, is named for its many Indian restaurants and spice shops."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-167]  "A Changing Neighborhood". Leo Baeck Institute. Retrieved November 22, 2022.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-168]  "A New African American Identity: The Harlem Renaissance". National Museum of African American History and Culture. Smithsonian. Retrieved September 13, 2023.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-LittleAustraliaNYC_169-0]  Shaun Busuttil (November 3, 2016). "G-day! Welcome to Little Australia in New York City". KarryOn. Archived from the original on May 23, 2019. Retrieved May 24, 2019. In Little Australia, Australian-owned cafes are popping up all over the place (such as Two Hands), joining other Australian-owned businesses (such as nightclubs and art galleries) as part of a growing green and gold contingent in NYC. Indeed, walking in this neighbourhood, the odds of your hearing a fellow Aussie ordering a coffee or just kicking back and chatting are high – very high – so much so that if you're keen to meet other Aussies whilst taking your own bite out of the Big Apple, then this is the place to throw that Australian accent around like it's going out of fashion!

^  [LINK: (a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-NYCBasics_170-0]   [LINK: (b) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-NYCBasics_170-1]  "NYC Basics". Archived from the original on October 11, 2007. Retrieved October 11, 2007.,  [LINK: (NYC & Company) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NYC_%26_Company] . Accessed June 30, 2009. "Downtown (below 14th Street) contains Greenwich Village, SoHo, TriBeCa, and the Wall Street financial district."

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan#cite_ref-171]  Grynbaum, Michael C. "Along 5th Ave. in Manhattan, a Dispute Over Where East and West Begin",  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times] , March 22, 2011. Accessed January 1, 2024. "Fifth Avenue, the glittering central spine of Manhattan, is the undisputed divider of the city's famous street grid: east of Fifth is East, and west of Fifth is West. Been that way since 1838."

